Observe that for an arbitrary polynomial  $P(x)=\sum_{i=0}^na_ix^i$ ,
\[\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}(P)}\frac{1}{r^2}=\left(\frac{a_1}{a_0}\right)^2-2\cdot\frac{a_2}{a_0},\]
derivable by computing  $\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}(Q)}r^2$  for  $Q(x):=P(\tfrac{1}{x})$  (which has the "reverse coefficients" of  $P$ ).

Now, because the  $f^n(x)$  are odd and so  $\tfrac{f^n(x)}{x}$  are even, so that  $a_1=0$  in the paradigm above, it suffices to keep track of the cubic  $a_3(n)$  and linear  $a_1(n)$  coefficients in the  $f^n(x)$ , which are the quadratic  $a_2'(n)$  and constant  $a_0'(n)$  coefficients in the  $\tfrac{f^n(x)}{x}$ , as needed.**Claim.**  $a_3(n)=(-3)^{n-1}\cdot\tfrac{9^n-1}{8}$  and  $a_1(n)=(-3)^n$ .**Proof.** The base case clearly holds; assume the assertion holds for some  $n=k$ . Consider
\begin{align*}
f^{k+1}(x)&=f(f^k(x))
&=f^k(x)^3-3f^k(x)
&=(\dots+a_3(k)x^3+a_1(k)x)^3-3(\dots+a_3(k)x^3+a_1(k)x)
&=(\dots+(-3)^{k-1}\cdot\tfrac{9^k-1}{8}\cdot x^3+(-3)^k\cdot x)^3-3(\dots+(-3)^{k-1}\cdot\tfrac{9^k-1}{8}\cdot x^3+(-3)^k\cdot x).
\end{align*}
Above, the first term contains terms all of degree  $>3$  except for the last,  $(-3)^{3k}\cdot x^3$ . Likewise, the second term contains terms of degree  $x>3$  except for the last two,  $(-3)^k\cdot\tfrac{9^k-1}{8}\cdot x^3$  and  $(-3)^{k+1}\cdot x$ . Accordingly,
\begin{align*}
a_3(k+1)&=(-3)^{3k}+(-3)^k\cdot\tfrac{9^k-1}{8}=(-3)^k\cdot\tfrac{9^{k+1}-1}{8}
a_1(k+1)&=(-3)^{k+1},
\end{align*}
which is the assertion for  $n=k+1$ .  $\Box$ So  $a_2'(2022)=(-3)^{2021}\cdot\tfrac{9^{2022}-1}{8}$  and  $a_0'(2022)=(-3)^{2022}$  are the quadratic and constant coefficients of  $\tfrac{f^{2022}(x)}{x}$ , which implies that (since  $a_2(n)=a_1'(n)=0$  for all  $n$  as previously noted)
\begin{align*}
\sum_{r\in\mathcal{R}}\frac{1}{r^2}&=-2\cdot\frac{a_2'(2022)}{a_0'(2022)}
&=-2\cdot\frac{(-3)^{2021}\cdot\tfrac{9^{2022}-1}{8}}{(-3)^{2022}}
&=\frac{3^{4044}-1}{12}.
\end{align*}
Ergo  $3+4044+1+12=4060$ , which contradicts the official answer of  $\boxed{4072}$ .**Motivation.** Of course,  $\tfrac{9^n-1}{8}=9^{n-1}+9^{n-2}+\dots+1$  was the actual form initially discovered; after staring at the smaller cases, I tried considering  $b(n)=\tfrac{a_3(n)=a_2'(n)}{3^{n-1}}$  and following an embarrassingly long time, finally noted that  $\Delta b(n)=9^n$  :oops:

 $P=\prod_{n=2}^{2022}\frac{2n+2}{2n+1}\implies \ln(P)=\sum_{n=2}^{2022}\ln\left(\frac{2n+2}{2n+1}\right)$ . 
Let  $f(x)=\ln\left(\frac{2n+2}{2n+1}\right)$ . As f(x) is decreasing for postive x's
\begin{align*}
\int_{a}^b f(x) dx \ge \sum_{x=a+1}^b f(x) \ge \int_{a+1}^{b+1} f(x) dx
\int_{1}^{2022} \ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right) dx \ge \sum_{x=2}^{2022} \ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right) \ge \int_{2}^{2023}\ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right) dx
\end{align*}
\[\int_{a}^{b} \ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right) dx=\frac{(2x+2)\ln(2x+2)-(2x+1)\ln(2x+1)-1}{2}\bigg]_{a}^{b}\]
\begin{align*}
I_1=\int_{1}^{2022}\ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right)=\frac{(4046\ln(4046)-4045\ln(4045))-(4\ln(4)-3\ln(3))}{2}
e^{I_1}=\sqrt{\frac{{4046}^{4046}3^3}{{4045}^{4045}4^4}}=\sqrt{4046\left(1+\frac{1}{4045}\right)^{4045}\frac{3^3}{4^4}}\ge \sqrt{4046e\frac{3^3}{4^4}}\approx  \sqrt{4046\frac{19}{7}\frac{3^3}{4^4}}\approx 34.03
e^{I_1}=34~\text{or}~35 \text{ or something like that}
I_2=\int_{2}^{2023}\ln\left(\frac{2x+2}{2x+1}\right)=\frac{(4048\ln(4048)-4047\ln(4047))-(6\ln(6)-5\ln(5))}{2}
e^{I_2}=\sqrt{\frac{{4048}^{4048}5^5}{{4047}^{4047}6^6}}=\sqrt{4048\left(1+\frac{1}{4047}\right)^{4047}\frac{5^5}{6^6}}\ge \sqrt{4048e\frac{5^5}{6^6}}\approx  \sqrt{4048\frac{19}{7}\frac{5^5}{6^6}}\approx 27.5
\implies e^{I_1}=27~\text{or}~28\text{ or something like that}
\end{align*}
Now  $e^{I_1}\ge P\ge e^{I_2}\implies P$  is between  $27$  and  $34$ . Hence  $\boxed{P=29}$  $\textbf{Remark: }$  This approach was only for fun, above's approach is much better. Also these are decent and it gets the job done  :thumbup:. Here you can take  $(1+\frac{1}{4046})^{4046}\approx e\approx \frac{19}{7}$  by the help of expansion of e

