By Euler's formula and the Taylor Series of  $-\ln(1-x)$ ,
\begin{align*}
A&=\text{Im}\left(\sum_{n=1}\frac{e^{ni}}{n}\right)
&=\text{Im}(-\ln(1-e^i))
&=\text{Im}(-\ln(1-\cos1-i\sin1)).
\end{align*}
We wish to convert  $1-\cos1-i\sin1$  into the form  $\cos\theta+i\sin\theta=e^{i\theta}$ , due to the natural logarithm. In particular we will have  $\theta=\arctan\tfrac{-\sin1}{1-\cos1}$ , but we must scale up by a factor of  $k=\sqrt{(1-\cos1)^2+(-\sin1)^2}$  (whose actual value, as shall see, is irrelevant). So we have
\begin{align*}
\text{Im}(-\ln(k(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)))&=\text{Im}(-\ln ke^{i\theta})
&=\text{Im}(-\ln k-i\theta)
&=-\theta.
\end{align*}
Finally, to simplify  $-\theta=\arctan\tfrac{\sin1}{1-\cos1}$ , we force it into the form  $\tfrac{\sin\theta'}{1+\cos\theta'}=\tan\tfrac{\theta'}{2}$ :
\begin{align*}
\arctan\tfrac{\sin1}{1-\cos1}&=\arctan\tan\tfrac{\sin(\pi-1)}{1+\cos(\pi-1)}
&=\arctan\tan\tfrac{\pi-1}{2}
&=\tfrac{\pi-1}{2}.
\end{align*}
Multiplying by  $100$  and flooring gives us  $\boxed{107}$ .

