Because  $f(x)=f'''(x)$ , write
\[f(x)=f(0)\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{x^{3k}}{(3k)!}+f'(0)\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{x^{3k+1}}{(3k+1)!}+f''(0)\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{x^{3k+2}}{(3k+2)!}.\]
By a RoU filter on  $e^x$ , each of the sums above is of the form  $\tfrac{e^x+a_1e^{\omega x}+a_2e^{\omega^2x}}{3}$ . So
\begin{align*}
f(x)&=\tfrac{f(0)+f'(0)+f''(0)}{3}\cdot e^x+b_1e^{\omega x}+b_2\cdot e^{\omega^2x}
&=b_1e^{\omega x}+b_2e^{\omega^2x}
&=c_1e^{-x/2}\cos\tfrac{x\sqrt{3}}{2}+c_2e^{-x/2}\sin\tfrac{x\sqrt{3}}{2}i.
\end{align*}
Ignore the  $i$ . Letting  $x=0$  gives  $f(0)=c_1$ , and letting  $x=0$  in the derivative yields  $f'(0)=-\tfrac{c_1}{2}+\tfrac{c_2\sqrt{3}}{2}$ , i.e.,  $c_2=c_1\sqrt{3}$ . Hence,  $f(x)=0$  iff  $c_1e^{-x/2}\cos\tfrac{x\sqrt{3}}{2}+c_1\sqrt{3}e^{-x/2}\sin\tfrac{x\sqrt{3}}{2}$ , which simplifies to  $\tan\tfrac{x\sqrt{3}}{2}=-\tfrac{\sqrt{3}}{3}$  unless  $c_1=0$ . Combining these two cases together, we find  $m(f)=\boxed{0,\tfrac{5\pi\sqrt{3}}{9}}$ .

