To maximize laziness, the trick for these kinds of problems is to check the extremes, as in a symmetric inequality. Here, those extremes are adding all the rows first (cp.  $x=k$ ,  $y=z=\dots=0$ ) and adding the rows and columns in alternating fashion (cp.  $x=y=\dots$ ). The former costs  $19\cdot1^2+19\cdot20^2=7619$ ; the latter costs
\begin{align*}
1^2+2^2+2^2+3^2+\dots+19^2+20^2&=2(1^2+\dots+20^2)-(1^2+20^2)
&=\tfrac{20\cdot21\cdot41}{3}-(1+400)
&=5740-401=\boxed{5339}.
\end{align*}

What a meme, they literally ran out of ideas and decided to wrap  $f(x,y,z)=x^2+y^2+z^2-3xyz$  into a problem. Specifically, this is the expansion and rearrangement of the second equation (with the RHS equalling  $0$ ), and because 
\begin{align*}
g(x,y,z)&=x^2+y^2+z^2-xy-yz-zx
&=\tfrac{(x-y)^2+(y-z)^2+(z-x)^2)}{2}
&=\tfrac{24yz}{2}=12yz,
\end{align*}
we have  $h(x,y,z)=x+y+z=0$  (since  $f=g\cdot h$ ). Letting  $x=-y-z$  in the first equation simplifies to  $y^2-3yz+z^2=0$ , which solves as  $\tfrac{y}{z}=\tfrac{3\pm\sqrt{5}}{2}$ . So if WLOG  $y,z=\tfrac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2},1$ , we have that  $|x|=\tfrac{5+\sqrt{5}}{2}$  and
\[\tfrac{x^2}{yz}=\frac{(\tfrac{5+\sqrt{5}}{2})^2}{\tfrac{3+\sqrt{5}}{2}}=\boxed{5}.\]

