To justify the existence of this post, here's the solution to 7, the most immature problem I've seen in a while:<details><summary>*</summary>an SMT organizer who said "oops we're short on problems" and decided to wrap  $\zeta(2)=\tfrac{\pi^2}{6}$  into a convoluted, intimidating problem—equivalently, an elementary schooler who just learned about the aforementioned equation and overexcitedly turned it into an "original" "thought-provoking" problem</details>
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\left(\sum_{j=1}^\infty(1+k)^{-j}\right)^2\right)^{-t}}&=\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\sum_{k=1}^\infty\frac{1}{k^2}\right)^{-t}}
&=\sqrt{6\sum_{t=1}^\infty\left(1+\frac{\pi^2}{6}\right)^{-t}}
&=\sqrt{6\cdot\frac{1}{\frac{\pi^2}{6}}}=\sqrt{\frac{36}{\pi^2}}=\boxed{\frac{6}{\pi}}.
\end{align*}

