<details><summary>p10</summary>Let the ellipse have center  $M$ , semimajor axis  $a$ , semiminor axis  $b$ , and foci that are  $2c$  apart. Scale the figure so that  $b=1$ . We are given  $a=F_1W=BC$  and  $F_1F_2=AC$ , so  $c=1$  and  $a=\sqrt{2}$ . Let  $P$  be the projection of  $C$  onto  $\overline{BM}$ , so that  $BP=CP=1$ . Now compress the figure horizontally until the ellipse becomes a circle. Let  $BV=x$  and  $CV=CW=y$ .

The compression factor was  $a:1 = \sqrt{2}$ , so  $BP=BM-CW=\sqrt{1+x^2}-y=1/\sqrt{2}$ . Also,  $BC=(x+y)^2=BP^2+CP^2=3/2$ . Thus  $x=1/\sqrt{2}$ ,  $y=\sqrt{3/2}-x$ , and  $BM=\sqrt{3/2}$ .

Let  $Q$  be the projection of  $V$  onto  $\overline{BM}$ . From  $\triangle MQV \sim \triangle MVB$  we get  $QM=\sqrt{2/3}$  and  $VQ=\sqrt{1/3}$ . Thus the horizontal distance between  $V$  and  $M$  before compression was  $QM\sqrt{2} = \sqrt{4/3}$ , so  $QF_2 = 1+\sqrt{4/3}$ . The vertical distance remains  $\sqrt{1/3}$ , so  $VF_2^2$  comes out to be  $8/3+2\sqrt{4/3}$ . By symmetry, this equals  $F_1X = F_1Y$  (in the non-compressed figure).

Finally,  $ZQ$  has height  $2$ , so its length is  $\tfrac{2}{QF_2}\cdot F_1Y$ . Thus  $[F_1QYZ] = \tfrac12\cdot F_1Y\cdot ZQ = 2\sqrt{4/3}$ , so after scaling  $AB=BC$  to its original length, we get  $\left(\tfrac{45}{\sqrt{2}}\right)^2\cdot 2\sqrt{4/3} = \boxed{2025\sqrt{4/3}}$ .</details>

