We assume that an ant is walking through this matrix.

We make the following important observation.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :**    The ant passes through exactly one entry of each up-right/down-left diagonal.</span>

*Proof :*    It is clear that the ant must pass through at least one entry of each such diagonal (each diagonal separates the board into two disconnected parts). Now, say the ant has reach an entry of a certain diagonal. All entries of the same diagonal to the left of him cannot be visited. All those to the right of him are above him and thus they cannot be visited either. Thus, the claim is valid.

Now, note that if we number the rows and columns of the grid  $1$  to  $n$  from left to right and top to bottom, then the entries of each diagonal have the same value for  $r+c$  (where  $r$  is the number of the row of the entry and  $c$  is the number of the column).

Now, it is well known that for positive integers  $a+b=n$  for a fixed  $n$ ,  $ab$  is maximum when  $a-b$  is minimum and  $ab$  is minimum when  $a-b$  is maximum. Thus, we can conclude that the minimum sum of labels in the path of an ant (sum of minimum value in each diagonal) is 
\[1+2+3+\dots + n + n + 2n + \dots + n^2 = \frac{n^3+2n^2-n}{2}\]
which is clearly achievable by the ant traversing along the first column up to the bottom and then traversing the bottom row until the bottom-right square.

Further, the maximum of labels in the path of an ant (sum of maximum value in each diagonal) is 
\begin{align*}
    1 + 2 + 4 + 6 + \dots + n(n-1) + n^2  &= (1^2+2^2+\dots + n^2) + (1\cdot 2  + \dots + (n-1)n)
    &= \frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{6} + \frac{2n(n-1)(n+1)}{6}
    &= \frac{(n+1)(4n^2-n)}{6}
    &= \frac{4n^3+3n^2-n}{6}
\end{align*}

