<details><summary>Motivation: Configurational geo; Utilize the specific intersections of the lines. Also, (DEF) is very crucial.</summary>First of all, when seeing this problem, I just thought that I have no chance of solving this at all; if this is very heavily configurational, and there's a definition of  $A'$  and  $D'$  which isn't naturally formed using the given points in the problem, then I'm just dead meat. To that end, I decided to focus on the easiest and friendliest thing in the problem, which were  $X,Y$  and  $Z$ . 
Also, this problem is also perpendicularly heavy, as the problem literally asked to prove the concurrence of four lines, of which all of them are constructed perpendicularly towards an absurd line. Then, to my luck,  $QR$  is definitely a radical axis of some pairs of circles, and  $A'D'$  too --- but, of course, I didn't know or had an idea of that in the outset. **S1 (5 points). "Hard Tech" 1: Midpoint construction to attach  $Y$  and  $Z$  into.** My first actual idea in handling those points were to consider the triangles  $\triangle ABE$  and  $\triangle ACF$ , and note that  $\angle BAY = \angle CAZ.$  I tried to force some spiral similarity or isogonal conjugate thing, but that doesn't line up quite well as  $YZ$  would need to intersect the sides of the triangle at  $A'$ . 
Because I have read something about Hagge circles before and that  $"\text{lots of midpoints}"$  = construct more midpoints, especially through sides, I was motivated to pin  $M_A,M_B$  and  $M_C$ . I quickly noticed the midline property and  $\angle M_AZF = 90^{\circ}$ , and that puts  $(HYZM_A)$  cyclic. I was very sure this is the path that needs travelling because the fact that  $D$ , one of  $D,E,F$  which are very important points to define  $D'$ , is concyclic to them.  $\textcolor{green}{\text{Section 1 End.}}$   **S2 (2.5 points). "Soft Tech" 1: Finding the "locus" of A'.** In my opinion, this is simple enough, as here I only used the given definition of  $A'$  to precisely construct and specify its role in the problem, which is as an intersection of the line  $\overline{YZ}$  (belonging to both the concyclics of  $(HYZM_AD)$  and  $XYZ$ , the "mysterious triangle") and the line  $\overline{BC}$ , which is also a link of  $(DEFM_A)$  to the circle we have just established. 
Before this I was tempted to specify the circumcircle of  $ABC$  into the equation, but I immediately discarded the notion of calculating  $A'B \cdot A'C$ , some values which are clearly not given/supported by the statements given in the problem.  $\textcolor{green}{\text{Section 2 End.}}$  **S3 (10 points). Hard Tech 2: Mixing and matching possible circle incidences, to bring QR down to earth.** Here is the point I was most stuck during I was working on this problem. I tried reflecting and transforming the pesky points  $Y$  and  $Z$  to fit nicer to the grand picture, such as considering the midpoint of  $AH$  as a member of the nine-point circle, or reflecting  $H$  across  $Y$  and  $Z$  to create something parallel (here's the reason I wasn't stuck on the last part later!) 
Also, one thing which also trapped me is the fact that  $H$  is the Miquel point of  $(HYXM_CF), (HXZM_BE)$  and  $(HYZM_AD)$ , something which was really attractive due to  $X$  being the projection of  $D$  to  $M_CM_B$ , and  $D$  itself is a nice point when  $M_AM_BM_C$  is considered as the reference triangle. I think I also looked at  $\triangle PQR$  for a bit too long (of all the candidates for special triangles in the problem, I had  $ABC$ ,  $DEF$ ,  $XYZ$  and  $PQR$  shortlisted), before I realized that  $\textbf{I really haven't defined P, yet!}$  
Seeing that specifying  $A'$  directly from the intersection of two radical axes is useful, I proceeded to do the same for  $P$ , and for that I would need to find a nice circle which passes through  $\overline{AD}$  ---  $P$  is formed by  $\overline{YZ}$  and  $\overline{AD}$ , after all! Not only did I found one, but there were two circles that passes through  $\overline{AD}$ , as mentioned in  $\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{\text{Claim 2.}}}$  Then this part of the struggle is over, as  $\overline{QR}$  can easily be defined now.  $\textcolor{green}{\text{Section 3 End.}}$ **S4 (5+ points). Soft Tech 2 ("Center bashing"): What to do after finding the good radical axes.** After re-analyzing what the problem wants us to prove (back to the beginning of this motivation rant!) I realized that the likely importance of radical axes is the relation of the centres to said radical axis, as the problem literally asks us to prove
\[
\begin{tabular}{c|c} $A \rightarrow QR$ & $B \rightarrow PR$   \hline $C \rightarrow PQ$ & $O \rightarrow A'D'$  
\end{tabular}
\]
concur. I had every reason to consider again the two circles  $(DEF)$  and  $(XYZ)$ , as the golden key to the concurrence point lies on  $A'D'$ , and also  $O$ . Coincidentally, my optimism was doubled due to the fact that the center of  $(DEF)$  is the midpoint from  $H$  to  $O$ , (arguably) the most important point and the most floating point in the problem, respectively. Dilating the statement that  $\overline{NO_{XYZ}} \perp \overline{A'D'}$ , the idea of  $2 \cdot O_{XYZ} - H$  being the concurrence point came to view.
The rest of the steps, in my opinion, is already motivated enough.  $\textcolor{green}{\text{Motivation End.}}$  **Final Comments.** Though being much easier than P3, or even P5 (in my subjective view, anyway) this isn't a bad problem (I wouldn't be motivated to write this otherwise) as it highlights one of the most important tricks in proving perpendicularity and midpoints, that is centers of circles and radical axes pair up quite well. I'd admit that maybe I found this much faster than expected due to a series of nice coincidences that brought me to the finish right after **Section 3.** 
In total, I would give this 22.5+ points that nicely rounds up to 25 points, which is worth as 25M after conversion to MOHS. Some steps of this problem is certainly easier than others, so that's the reason why I think that this is a medium problem.</details>

