I didn't expect this to be so short and so meta, making this to be the most unexpected solution in both USEMO 2019 and 2020 for me (except for that Nikolai problem --- but aren't all Nikolai problems like that?) Slightly different than the above posts, but still the same core idea. 
Denote the colors by  $1,2$  and  $3$ . Our objective is to prove that if the amount of edges (a.k.a. consecutive pairs when arrange cyclically) with different-colored endpoints are even, then there exists consecutive terms (arranged cyclically) with equal number of vertices of each color.  $\color{green}\rule{25cm}{3pt}$  $\textcolor{green}{\textbf{\text{Transiting to geometry.}}}$  Place Ankan in a vertex of the polygon (WLOG with side  $1$ ), and he walks clockwise along the polygon. Ankan brings a sheet of Cartesian plane with him, and marks the point  $a_0 = (0,0)$  in it. If after  $i$  units of walking he passes through a vertex of color  $1$ , he marks the point  $a_{i+1} = a_i + (0,1)$ . Similarly he marks  $a_{i+1} = a_i + (1,0)$  if he passes through a color  $2$ , but he will mark  $a_{i+1} = a_i + (-1,-1)$  when he passes through a color  $3$ . He will do that until he returns to his initial starting point after  $n$  units of walking.
After that Ankan pulls an edge across all  $\overline{a_ia_{i+1}}, 0 \leq i \leq n-1.$  ( $a_{n} = a_0$  as usual.) Then there exists a patriotic set if and only if the polygon in Ankan's Cartesian sheet is self-intersecting.  $\color{green}\rule{25cm}{0.5pt}$  $\textcolor{green}{\textbf{\text{Proof 0.}}}$  If Ankan's Cartesian polygon is self-intersecting, then it can be divided to at least  $2$  non-intersecting polygons. However, a closed polygon represents a patriotic set (also note that since the initial  $n$  points which form a patriotic set implies that Ankan's Cartesian polygon is closed, too.) and so we can just take the consecutive vertices to be the ones that form one of the partitioned non-intersecting polygons. 
Likewise, if Ankan's Cartesian polygon is not self-intersecting, meaning that it has no  $"\text{sub closed-loops}"$ , there's no way Ankan can partition the initial polygon into two patriotic sets which are consecutive, as their existence implies an existence of a closed-loop in Ankan's Cartesian polygon.  $\blacksquare$  $\color{blue}\rule{25cm}{1pt}$   $\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{\text{The Claim.}}}$  Every non-patriotic set will produce an odd-sided polygon when it is subjected to Ankan's procedure.  $\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{\text{The Proof.}}}$  Proceed by induction. Consider the interior angles of the polygon. We can observe that they can only be of  $\{45^{\circ},90^{\circ},270^{\circ},315^{\circ}\}$ . If there are no interior angles of big size, then we can force a contradiction by computing their sum (which are roughly  $180n$ .) --- except when  $n=3$ . 
If there exists a big interior angle, we pick an angle and extend the two sides forming that angle; and intersect them with the first (non-parallel) side they meet. 

