Call the two sides  $s_1$  and  $s_2$ , and their extended sides cut short to their first intersection to be  $S_1$  and  $S_2$ . Note that this partitions Ankan's polygon  $\mathcal{P}$  into three connected components  $\mathcal{C}_1, \mathcal{C}_2$  (the corner ones) and  $\mathcal{C}_3$  (the middle one).  
Consider three polygons  $\mathcal{P}_1$ ,  $\mathcal{P}_2$  and  $\mathcal{P}_3$  bounded by  $(S_1)$ ,  $(S_2)$  and  $(S_1, S_2)$ ; or in the analytical sense, let  $\mathcal{P}_1 = \mathcal{C}_1 \cup \mathcal{C}_3$ ,  $\mathcal{P}_2 = \mathcal{C}_2 \cup \mathcal{C}_3$ , and  $\mathcal{P}_3 = \mathcal{C}_3$ .  $\color{red}\rule{25cm}{2pt}$ Then, it is easily verifiable that when we count the sides of 
\[\mathcal{P}_1+\mathcal{P}_2-\mathcal{P}_3 \]
it is equal to the sides of  $\mathcal{P} - 2$  ( $-2$  due to the extensions  $s_1 \rightarrow S_1$  and  $s_2 \rightarrow S_2$  do not produce additional sides, while we do have to substract with the sides  $S_1-s_1$  and  $S_2-s_2$  which come up in  $\mathcal{P}_3$ .) Then, roughly speaking, since the sides of each  $\mathcal{P}_i$  is strictly less than  $n$ , we can proceed by induction to ensure that 
\[ \mathcal{P} = \mathcal{P}_1+\mathcal{P}_2-\mathcal{P}_3 + 2 \]
