Had a big hint to use roots of unity. This problem is instructive enough that I'm still writing this solution for storage.

Pick  $n>\deg f, \deg g, \deg h$ , and let  $z$  be a primitive  $n$ th root of unity. The key claim is the following:**<span style="color:#00f">Claim:</span>** If  $p$  is a real-coefficient polynomial with  $\deg p < n$ . Then we have  $$ S(p)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |f(z^i)|^2. $$  <span style="color:#00f">Proof:</span> Note that for any complex  $w$  we have  $p(\overline{w})=\overline{p(w)}$ , which comes from the fact that  $\overline{w}^k=\overline{w^k}$ . Hence  $$ \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |p(z^i)|^2=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} p(z^i)p(z^{-i}), $$  so it suffices to prove that the RHS is equal to  $S(p)$ . Write  $p(x)=a_kx^k+a_{k-1}x^{k-1}+\cdots+a_1x+a_0$  where  $k=\deg p < n$ . Then, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} p(z^i)p(z^{-i})&=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{a=0}^{n-1}\sum_{0 \leq i,j \leq k-1} a_ia_jz^{a(i-j)}
&=\sum_{a=0}^{n-1}\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{k-1} a_i^2+\sum_{a=0}^{n-1}\frac{2}{n}\sum_{0 \leq i<j\leq k-1} a_ia_jz^{a(i-j)}
&=\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}a_i^2+\frac{2}{n}\sum_{0 \leq i<j\leq k-1}a_ia_j\sum_{a=0}^{n-1} z^{a(i-j)}.
\end{align*}
Since  $k \geq i-j \geq -k$  and  $i-j \neq 0$ , it follows that  $n \nmid i-j$ , so  $$ \sum_{a=0}^{n-1} z^{a(i-j)}=0, $$  which implies the second term is  $0$  and  $$ S(p)=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} p(z^i)p(z^{-i}) $$  as desired.  $\blacksquare$ Since  $f(x)g(x)=h(x)^2$ , the desired inequality is equivalent to  $$ \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |f(z^i)|^2\right)\left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |g(z^i)|^2\right)\geq \left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |h(z^i)|^2\right)^2=\left(\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} |f(z^i)||g(z^i)|\right)^2 $$  as  $|h(z^i)|^2=|h(z^i)^2|$ , but this is just Cauchy-Schwarz so we're done.  $\blacksquare$ **<span style="color:#00f">Remark (motivation):</span>** Rather than use the fact that  $$ a_k=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\omega^{-ki}f(\omega^i) $$  directly, my motivation for coming up with the expression for  $S(p)$  was to use the fact that we were looking for the sum of the coefficients in  $p(x)p(y)$  where the exponents of  $x$  and  $y$  were equal. Setting  $y=\tfrac{1}{x}$  means that we just want the constant value of  $f(x)f(\tfrac{1}{x})$ , but plugging in zero directly is illegal, so we use a roots of unity filter which is the next best thing.

