The answer is  $\alpha = 3/2$ . Unsurprisingly, we will split the solution into two parts.
------**Construction:** We use the "sqrt decomposition" trick known in competitive programming. The following diagram is for all  $n\leq 2\cdot(5^2+1)$ .
[asy]
size(8cm,0);
int k=5; int n=k*k;
void rect(int a, int b){
	fill(shift(a,b)*((0,0)--(0,1)--(1,1)--(1,0)--cycle),green);
}
for(int i=0; i<n; ++i){
	for(int j=0; j<n; ++j){
    	if((i%k==k-1) || (j%k==k-1) || i<k || j<k) rect(i,j);
    }
}
pen p = gray;
for(int i=0; i<=n; ++i){
    if(i<=k){
		draw((0,i)--(n,i) ^^ (i,0)--(i,n),p);
    }
}
for(int i=k+1; i<=n; ++i){
	draw((i,0)--(i,k) ^^ (0,i)--(k,i),p);
    for(int j=0; j<k-1; ++j){
    	draw((2k-1+k*j,i)--(2k+k*j,i),p);
        draw((i,2k-1+k*j)--(i,2k+k*j),p);
    }
}
for(int i=0; i<k-1; ++i){
	draw((2k-1+k*i,k)--(2k-1+k*i,n),p);
    draw((2k+k*i,k)--(2k+k*i,n),p);
    draw((k,2k-1+k*i)--(n,2k-1+k*i),p);
    draw((k,2k+k*i)--(n,2k+k*i),p);
}
[/asy]
More formally, let  $k = \lceil \sqrt{n}\rceil$ . Then we select the cell  $(x,y)$  such that either  $x$  or  $y$  is in the set  $$ S = \{1,2,\dots,k\} \cup \{2k,3k,\dots, k\cdot k\}. $$  It's easy to check that  $\{a-b : a,b\in S\} = \{0,1,2,\dots,k^2-1\}$ . It then becomes clear that the selected cells cover all bagel of order  $n$ , taking  $4k^3 \sim 4n^{3/2}$  cells.
------**Bound:** First, we alter the problem slightly by *not permitting* rotations by adding the rotated copy of the bakery to itself. Although this increases the size by twice, it will be fine.

Now, for each bagel that takes at least  $n/4$  rows, we color both top and bottom rows red. Thus, each red row has at least  $n/4$  cells. Now, we claim that**<span style="color:red">Claim:</span>** There are at least  $(1+o(1))\sqrt{n/2}$  red rows.

*Proof:* For any integer  $x \in [2, n/4]$ , we must be able to find two red rows with separation at least  $x$ . Thus, if  $k$  is the number of red rows, then  $\tbinom k2 \geq \tfrac n4-2$ , which implies the result.  $\blacksquare$ Thus, there are at least  $\left(\tfrac{1}{4\sqrt{2}} + o(1)\right) n^{3/2}$  cells, giving the desired bound.

