- Additionally, at most  $4(n-2)$  of the outer edges can be removed 	since there are  $4(n-2)$  lattice points within one unit of the boundary.
 Hence, the minimum number of removed edges is at least \[ n^2 - \left(\left\lfloor\frac{1}{2}(n-3)^2\right\rfloor+4(n-2)\right) \] which equals the claimed minimum.

I did graph theory, it's probably equivalent to the other global solutions but it made the most sense to me at the time because of the given conditions. I claim the answer is  $4+\frac{2022\cdot 2020}{2}$ .

Consider a graph where vertices are sticks and edges are between neighboring sticks. This graph is planar, and the amount of edges it has are the amount of sides of cells that are borders between sticks, because every two sticks has at most one shared unit of perimeter. There are  $2\cdot 2022\cdot 2021$  sides of cells that are inside the grid, and each stick of area  $a$  covers  $a-1$  of them. Since the total area of the sticks is  $2022^2$ , there are  $2\cdot 2022\cdot 2021-2022^2+m=m+2022\cdot 2020$  sides of cells that are borders between sticks, or  $m+2022\cdot 2020$  edges in the graph.

You can also draw an edge around the grid connecting something bordering the left of the grid with something bordering the right while preserving planarity, so there exists a planar graph with  $m+2022\cdot 2020+1$  edges and  $m$  vertices.  $m+2022\cdot 2020+1\le 3m-6$ , so  $m\ge 4+\frac{2022\cdot 2020}{2}$ , proving the lower bound.

