It follows that   $$  \sin \theta_A \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_A} + \sin \theta_B \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_B} + \sin \theta_C \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_C} = 0 \qquad \qquad (\star)  $$   $(\star)$  is actually sufficient to impply  $P,P_A,P_B,P_C$  are concyclic (!). The proof is just \textit{Ptolemy's Theorem}. We prove this formally as a Lemma:**<span style="color:#f00">Lemma: </span>** Let  $\ell_A,\ell_B,\ell_C$  be three lines concurrent at  $P$ . Let  $\theta_A$  denote value of non-obtuse angle between  $\ell_B$  and  $\ell_C$ . Define  $\theta_B,\theta_C$  similarly. Lengths are directed on  $\ell_A,\ell_B,\ell_C$  such that among the three rays formed by positive direction through  $P$ , no ray lies inside the angle formed by the other two rays. 
Then  $$ \sin \theta_A \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_A} + \sin \theta_B \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_B} + \sin \theta_C \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_C} = 0 \qquad \qquad (\star) $$  if and only if points  $P,P_A,P_B,P_C$  are concyclic. 

*Proof:* Suppose  $P,P_A,P_B,P_C$  are concyclic. WLOG points  $P,P_B,P_A,P_C$  lie on a circle in that order. 
Ptolemy's Theorem gives  $$  PP_A \cdot P_BP_C = PP_B \cdot P_CP_A + PP_C \cdot P_AP_B $$  By law of sines,   $$  P_BP_C : P_CP_A : P_AP_B = \sin \theta_A : \sin \theta_B : \sin \theta_C  $$  So we obtain 
\begin{align}
    \sin \theta_A \cdot PP_A = \sin \theta_B \cdot PP_B + \sin \theta_C \cdot PP_C
\end{align}
Now  $P_A$  lies inside  $\angle P_BPP_C$ , so  $\ell_A$  passes through interior of  $\angle P_BPP_C$ . This means  $\overrightarrow{PP_B},\overrightarrow{PP_C}$  have the same sign, WLOG both are non-negaitve. Now  $\overrightarrow{PP_A}$  must be non-positive, otherwise  $P$  lies inside  $\triangle P_AP_BP_C$ . It follows 
\begin{align*}
    \sin \theta_A (- \overrightarrow{PP_A}) = \sin \theta_B (\overrightarrow{PP_B}) + \sin \theta_C ( \overrightarrow{PP_C}) 
    \implies \sin \theta_A \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_A} + \sin \theta_B \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_B} + \sin \theta_C \cdot \overrightarrow{PP_C} = 0
\end{align*}
So  $(\star)$  holds true. Now the converse direction just follows from a Phantom point argument.  $\blacksquare$

