The condition in the problem is equivalent to proving that  $\overline{B_2 C_2} \parallel \overline{BC}$  and likewise conditions on the other two sides.

To prove this we use power of a point: We have that  $\angle B_2 E_1 B_1 = \angle B_2 E B_1 = 90^{\circ}$ , so  $B_2 E_1 EB_1$  is cyclic. The diagonals intersect at  $H$ , so
\[ E_1 H \cdot HB_1 = B_2H \cdot HE. \qquad (\heartsuit)\]
Similarly,  $C_2 F_1 FC_1$  is cyclic, so
\[ F_1 H \cdot HC_1 = C_2 H \cdot HF. \qquad (\diamondsuit)\]

We also have  $\angle C_1E_1 B_1 = \angle C_1 F_1 B_1 = 90^{\circ}$ , so  $C_1E_1F_1B_1$  is cyclic. Then,
\[ F_1 H \cdot HC_1 = E_1H \cdot HB_1. \]
Substituting the LHS with  $(\diamondsuit)$  and the RHS with  $(\heartsuit)$ , we have
\[ C_2H \cdot HF = B_2 H \cdot HE.\]
Dividing both sides by  $CH \cdot HF=BH \cdot HE$  (because  $BFEC$  is cyclic), we have
\[  \frac{C_2H}{CH} = \frac{B_2H}{BH} \implies \overline{B_2C_2} \parallel \overline{BC},\]
as desired.

Let  $A_3$ ,  $B_3$ , and  $C_3$  be the feet of the perpendiculars from  $A$ ,  $B$ , and  $C$  to the respective sides of  $\triangle ABC$ . In addition, denote  $A_4$ ,  $B_4$ , and  $C_4$  be the feet of the perpendiculars from  $A_1$ ,  $B_1$ , and  $C_1$  to the respective sides of  $\triangle A_1B_1C_1$ .

Notice,  $AB_3A_3B$ ,  $A_1A_2A_3A_4$ , and  $B_1B_2B_3B_4$  are cyclic. Hence, by power of point at  $H$ :
\begin{align*}
\dfrac{HA}{HB} = \dfrac{HB_3}{HA_3} = \dfrac{(HB_1 \cdot HB_4) / (HB_2)}{(HA_1 \cdot HA_4) / (HA_2)} = \dfrac{HA_2}{HB_2} \cdot \dfrac{HB_1 \cdot HB_4}{HA_1 \cdot HA_4}
\end{align*}
But,  $A_1B_4A_4B_1$  is cyclic, thus the above result implies  $\tfrac{HA}{HB} = \tfrac{HA_2}{HB_2}$ . Hence,  $A_2B_2 \parallel AB$ . Thus,  $H$  is the orthocenter of  $A_2B_2C_2$ , as desired.

