The answer is no. Impose a coordinate system such that the centers of the unit cubes are located at  $(x,y,z)$  for integers  $1 \le x,y,z \le n$ . Assume FTSOC that the half-space containing the positive numbers is given by  $ax+by+cz+d>0$ . Let  $f(x,y,z)$  denote the number written at  $(x,y,z)$ . The problem statement gives us the equations
\begin{align*}
\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le y,z \le n}} f(i,y,z)&=0 
\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le x,z \le n}} f(x,j,z)&=0 
\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le x,y \le n}} f(x,y,k)&=0
\end{align*}
for integers  $1 \le i,j,k \le n$ . We linearly combine them in the following way: we have
\begin{align*}
0&=\sum_{i=1}^n (ai+d)\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le y,z \le n}} f(i,y,z)+\sum_{j=1}^n bj\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le x,z \le n}} f(x,j,z)+\sum_{k=1}^n ck\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le x,y \le n}} f(x,y,k) 
&=\sum_{\mathclap{1 \le x,y,z \le n}} (ax+by+cz+d)f(x,y,z).
\end{align*}
However,  $ax+by+cz+d$  is nonzero and has the same sign as  $f(x,y,z)$ , so the sum is positive, a contradiction.  $\square$

