Let  $n \ge 3$  be an integer and consider an  $n \times n$  grid of unit squares.  Elaine wishes to paint each of the  $n^2$  squares of the grid either white or black such that the following three conditions all hold:
(i)  Of the  $4n-4$  squares touching the edge of the grid, the four corners are black and the other  $4n - 8$  squares are white.
(ii)  No two black squares share an edge.
(iii)  For every two distinct white squares  $w$  and  $w'$ , there is exactly one white path starting with  $w$  and ending with  $w'$ .
[asy]
   size(3.5cm);
    pen shade = darkgrey;
    fill(shift(0,5)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(5,5)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(0,0)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(5,0)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(4,4)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(2,4)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(1,3)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(4,2)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(2,2)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(3,1)*unitsquare, shade);
    fill(shift(1,1)*unitsquare, shade);
    for (int i=1; i<6; ++i) {
      draw( (i,0)--(i,6), black );
      draw( (0,i)--(6,i), black );
    }
    draw(box((0,0), (6,6)), black+1.5);
[/asy]
Here, a *white path* refers to a finite sequence  $(w_0, w_1, \dots, w_m)$  of distinct white cells such that  $w_i$  and  $w_{i+1}$  share an edge for all  $0 \le i \le m-1$ .  One example of such a coloring when  $n = 6$  is shown above.

Prove that there are infinitely many integers  $n$  for which Elaine can accomplish the task, and infinitely many for which she cannot.

Let  $n \ge 2$  be an even integer and let  $T$  be a tree with  $n$  vertices.  Show that  $T$  has an edge between two vertices whose degrees are either both even or both odd.

(Here, a *tree* is defined as a set of  $n$  *vertices* and  $n-1$  *edges* between pairs of vertices, such that any two vertices are linked via a path of one or more edges.  The *degree* of a vertex is defined as the number of edges using that vertex.  For example, if  $n=6$  and  $T$  is a tree with  $5$  edges  $ab$ ,  $bc$ ,  $cd$ ,  $ce$ , and  $cf$  as illustrated below, then edge  $bc$  joins two even-degree vertices:  $b$  has degree 2 while  $c$  has degree 4.)
[asy]
  dotfactor *= 1.5;
  unitsize(1.2cm);
  pair a = (0,0);
  pair b = (1,0.4);
  pair c = (2,0);
  pair d = (3,0.4);
  pair e = (1.3,-0.4);
  pair f = (2.7,-0.4);
  dot(" $a$ ", a, dir(90));
  dot(" $b$ ", b, dir(90));
  dot(" $c$ ", c, dir(90));
  dot(" $d$ ", d, dir(90));
  dot(" $e$ ", e, dir(180));
  dot(" $f$ ", f, dir(0));
  draw(a--b--c--d);
  draw(e--c--f);
[/asy]

solution_md:
The answer is  $4051$ , generally  $2m+1$  for  $2025$  replaced by  $m$ .Clearly we may assert that  $\mathcal{S}$  is convex, since otherwise any convex  $2025$ -gon containing  $\mathcal{T}$  the convex hull will inevitably contain any points strictly inside the convex hull as well, rendering  $\mathcal{T}$  the convex hull unattainable.

The key idea is to consider ``clusters'' of consecutive vertices that are excluded from  $\mathcal{T}$ . Observe that each cluster must be separated from the rest of the points by some side of the  $2025$ -gon. Further, no two clusters can be separated from the rest of the points by the same side of the  $2025$ -gon. As long as these conditions hold, we can let these separating lines meet to become the sides of our desired  $2025$ -gon (adding as many negligible sides as are necessary).
[asy]
        size(7.5cm);
pair p(int i) {
  return dir(24*i);
}

int[] l = {0,3,8,11,13,14};

for(int i = 0; i < l.length; ++i) {
  draw(circle(p(l*),0.05));
}

for (int i = 0; i < 15; ++i) {
  dot(p(i));
}

pair A = p(3)+(0,0.12);
pair B = p(8)-(0.12,0);
pair C = p(11)-(0.03,0.07);
pair D = (1.5,-0.55);

draw(A--B--C--D--cycle, red);
[/asy]
When  $\mathcal{S}$  is a convex  $4051$ -gon, each subset  $\mathcal{T}$  produces at most  $2025$  clusters, and so each can have its own separating side, giving the desired  $2025$ -gon that only covers  $\mathcal{T}$ . On the other hand, if  $\mathcal{S}$  has at least  $4052$  vertices, the alternating subset  $\mathcal{T}$  has at least  $2026$  clusters, impossible.  $\blacksquare$ **Remarks.** The above problem is classical in the field of [i]Vapnik–Chervonenkis theory*. A set  $\mathcal{S}$  is said to be *shattered* by a collection  $\mathcal{C}$  of sets if, for each  $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{S}$ , there exists some  $\mathcal{C}_i\in\mathcal{C}$  such that  $\mathcal{C}_i\cap\mathcal{S}=\mathcal{T}$ . Then the *Vapnik–Chervonenkis dimension* of a collection  $\mathcal{C}$  is the cardinality of the largest set it shatters. For example, the above problem shows that the VC dimension of closed convex  $m$ -gons in  $\mathbb{R}^2$  is  $2m+1$ .

The VC dimension has applications in neural networks and statistical neural theory. For example, the collection of half-planes in  $\mathbb{R}^2$  can classify at most  $3$  points; and generally, the VC dim. of a classification model reflects its ``capacity" or ``complexity".

