<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $a,b,c$  be the complex number representations of  $A,B,C$  respectively. Then,  $B_1=\frac{a+2c}{3}$  and  $B_2=\frac{2a+c}{3}$ . By rotation with center  $B_2$ ,
\begin{align*}
B_3&=e^{\frac{i\pi}{3}}(\frac{c-a}{3})+\frac{2a+c}{3}
&=(\frac{2}{3}-\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3})a+(\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3}+\frac{1}{3})c
\end{align*}
Analogously,
\begin{align*}
C_3&=(\frac{2}{3}-\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3})b+(\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3}+\frac{1}{3})a
&=(\frac{2}{3}-\frac{\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i}{3})b+(\frac{\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}i}{3}+\frac{1}{3})a
&=(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{6}i)b+(\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{6}i)a
\end{align*}
A rotation with centered at the centroid,  $G=\frac{a+b+c}{3}$  will map  $B_3$  as follows.
\begin{align*}
r(B_3)&=e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}((\frac{1}{3}-\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3})a+\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3}c+\frac{b}{3})+\frac{a+b+c}{3}
&=e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}(\frac{1}{3}-\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3})a+e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}\frac{e^{\frac{\pi i}{3}}}{3}c+\frac{e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}}{3}b+\frac{a+b+c}{3}
&=(\frac{e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}}{3}+\frac{2}{3})a+(\frac{1}{3}-\frac{e^{\frac{2\pi i}{3}}}{3})b
&=(\frac{1}{2}+\frac{\sqrt{3}}{6}i)a+(\frac{1}{2}-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{6}i)b=C_3
\end{align*}
Therefore,  $\triangle B_3GC_3$  is an isosceles triangle with vertex angle  $\angle G=120^\circ$ .  $\triangle A_3GB_3$  and  $\triangle C_3GA_3$  are isosceles triangles with the same vertex angle. By SAS congruence,  $\triangle B_3GC_3\cong\triangle A_3GB_3\cong\triangle C_3GA_3$ , and  $A_3B_3\cong B_3C_3\cong C_3A_3$ , which shows that  $\triangle A_3B_3C_3$  is equilateral, as desired.</details>

